{"name":"Sapphire Stone","hex":"#41495d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sapphire","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A clear deep blue variety of corundum, valued as a precious stone.","A white, yellow, or purple variety of corundum, either clear or translucent."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["of a deep blue colour.","pertaining to a 45th year"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sapphire"},{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sapphire","blocks":[{"text":"Sapphire is a gemstone variety of the mineral corundum, the crystalline form of aluminium oxide (α-Al2O3). Pure corundum is colorless, while trace elements in its crystal structure produce a wide range of colors. Blue is the best-known, but sapphires also occur in yellow, green, purple, pink, orange, gray, black, and colorless forms. Red gem-quality corundum is classified as ruby rather than sapphire. The boundary between ruby and pink sapphire is based on color appearance and varies among gemological and trade conventions. Sapphire has a Mohs hardness of 9, making it highly resistant to scratching and abrasion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Logan_Sapphire_10956420_cropped.png/330px-Logan_Sapphire_10956420_cropped.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sapphire","wikidata":"Q127583"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)","wikidata":"Q8063"},{"title":"Shades of blue","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color blue may differ in hue, chroma, or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a blue or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Shades_of_Blue.svg/330px-Shades_of_Blue.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_blue","wikidata":"Q7460342"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg/500px-Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg/1280px-Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg/500px-Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Blue corundum crystals in weathered feldspar in an alkaline pegmatite from the Canaã massif, Brazil.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlkaline_pegmatite.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Sapphire_%28GeoDIL_number_-_1432%29.jpg/500px-Sapphire_%28GeoDIL_number_-_1432%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Sapphire_%28GeoDIL_number_-_1432%29.jpg/1280px-Sapphire_%28GeoDIL_number_-_1432%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Sapphire_%28GeoDIL_number_-_1432%29.jpg/500px-Sapphire_%28GeoDIL_number_-_1432%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rough sapphire crystals of several colors from near Mogok, Myanmar.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASapphire_(GeoDIL_number_-_1432).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/The_Star_of_Lanka_%285784831032%29.jpg/500px-The_Star_of_Lanka_%285784831032%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/The_Star_of_Lanka_%285784831032%29.jpg/500px-The_Star_of_Lanka_%285784831032%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The six-rayed star of the Star of Lanka sapphire","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Star_of_Lanka_(5784831032).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%9C%E8%93%9D%E5%AE%9D%E7%9F%B3%E7%A0%82%E7%9F%BF%E5%BC%80%E9%87%87.jpg/500px-%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%9C%E8%93%9D%E5%AE%9D%E7%9F%B3%E7%A0%82%E7%9F%BF%E5%BC%80%E9%87%87.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/%E5%B1%B1%E4%B8%9C%E8%93%9D%E5%AE%9D%E7%9F%B3%E7%A0%82%E7%9F%BF%E5%BC%80%E9%87%87.jpg/500px-%E5%B1